      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We can use Euler's theorem for this purpose, which states that if \\(\\gcd(a, n) = 1\\), then \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}\\), where \\(\\phi\\) is the Euler's totient function.\n\nFirst, we need to compute \\(\\phi(1000)\\):\n\\[\n1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi(1000) = 1000 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 1000 \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{4}{5} = 400\n\\]\nSince \\(\\gcd(5, 1000) = 5\\), Euler's theorem tells us that \\(5^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\).\n\nThus, we need to reduce the exponent 1993 modulo 400:\n\\[\n1993 \\mod 400 = 1993 - 4 \\times 400 = 1993 - 1600 = 393\n\\]\nSo, \\(5^{1993} \\equiv 5^{393} \\pmod{1000}\\).\n\nNow, we need to compute \\(5^{393} \\mod 1000\\). We can use successive squaring to do this efficiently:\n\\[\n5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n5^4 = (5^2)^2 = 25^2 = 625\n\\]\n\\[\n5^8 = (5^4)^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{16} = (5^8)^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{32} = (5^{16})^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{64} = (5^{32})^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{128} = (5^{64})^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{256} = (5^{128})^2 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n5^{393} = 5^{256+128+8+5+3} = 5^{256} \\times 5^{128} \\times 5^8 \\times 5^5 \\times 5^3 = 625 \\times 625 \\times 625 \\times 3125 \\times 125 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\nWe can simplify this step by step:\n\\[\n625 \\times 625 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n625 \\times 625 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n625 \\times 3125 = 1953125 \\equiv 125 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\\[\n125 \\times 125 = 15625 \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\nThus, \\(5^{1993} \\equiv 625 \\pmod{1000}\\).\n\nTherefore, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{625}\\).",
