      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to determine \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We will use properties of modular arithmetic and the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to simplify this calculation.\n\nFirst, we break down the problem using the Chinese Remainder Theorem by dealing with the moduli \\(1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\\). We need to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\) and \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\) and then combine the results.\n\n### Step 1: Compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\)\nSince \\(5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), we have:\n\\[5^1 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8,\\]\n\\[5^2 \\equiv 25 \\equiv 1 \\mod 8.\\]\nThus, for any odd exponent \\(k\\), \\(5^k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\). Since 1993 is odd, we have:\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 8.\\]\n\n### Step 2: Compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\)\nSince \\(1993 > 3\\), we can use Euler's theorem, which states \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\mod n\\) for \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) coprime. Here, \\(\\phi(125) = 125 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 100\\), so:\n\\[5^{100} \\equiv 1 \\mod 125.\\]\nThus, we can reduce the exponent modulo 100:\n\\[1993 \\mod 100 = 93.\\]\nSo,\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5^{93} \\mod 125.\\]\nWe need to compute \\(5^{93} \\mod 125\\). We can use repeated squaring:\n\\[5^2 = 25,\\]\n\\[5^4 = 25^2 = 625 \\equiv 625 - 5 \\times 125 = 625 - 625 = 0 \\mod 125.\\]\nThus,\n\\[5^{93} = 5^{4 \\cdot 23 + 1} = (5^4)^{23} \\cdot 5 \\equiv 0^{23} \\cdot 5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 125.\\]\nSo,\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 125.\\]\n\n### Step 3: Combine the results using the Chinese Remainder Theorem\nWe have:\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 8,\\]\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 125.\\]\nSince both congruences are satisfied, we can combine them using the Chinese Remainder Theorem. The solution to the system of congruences is:\n\\[x \\equiv 5 \\mod 1000.\\]\nThus, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are:\n\\[\\boxed{012}.\\]",
